A significant aspect of musical culture in human traditions is the performance 
of existing music, often stored in a physical medium such as sheet music. This 
element of musical culture is present in the robots of _Stray_, and this fact 
most obviously communicated through the character of Morusque.

Morusque can be met in "The Slums", a run down village located at the bottom 
of the walled city, both physically and socially. Around the slums, various 
pages of sheet music can be found and brought to Morusque who will then 
perform a rendition of the music on his guitar @gameplay[1:05:05]. The name 
"Morusque" is significant because it is also an alias used by Yann Van der 
Cruyssen, the composer for all of the music in _Stray_ @sn. This suggests that 
the character acts as something of a self-insert for the game's core musical 
artist. This lends a sense of humanity to the character by transposing the 
personality and passions of a real-world person onto an otherwise soulless 
narrative-world robot.

Before diving into the significance of his playing and the individual pieces, 
there are a few items of note about Morusque's playing style. First, the 
guitar he plays on appears to be fashioned out of a gas can. There is also no 
visible amplifier circuitry, which further suggests that the instrument is 
acoustic. However, the diegetic audio we hear is has the sound of a MIDI 
guitar, notable for its consistent timbre and attack and lack of noise from 
the frets and strings. While this could be due to conserve system memory or 
effort during development, the high fidelity of the rest of the games audio 
and visuals suggests that this is an intentional stylistic choice to convey 
robotic quality to the performance. Additionally, Morusque's playing animation 
consists of a repetitive strumming motion, even during musical segments that 
suggest a more sparse melody or a finger-style technique. In an interview, Van 
der Cruyssen justifies this stylistic approach as an imitation of human music, 
now appearing "distant" and "rusty" @sn.

The not-quite-human style of the instrumentalist are even more present in the
individual pieces that he performs. The first of the numbered sheets of music
is titled "Petite valse" and is a waltz played in a classical guitar style
@gameplay[1:47:53]. The piece bears a striking resemblance to to music of
Spanish classical guitarist Francisco Tárrega, especially his well known
composition _Gran Vals_. By alluding to human classical music, the game music
suggests that inspiration has been drawn from the classical music of human in
the creation of new music by the robots. Another performance, "Tomorrows", is
notable as one of the songs that can be heard playing on various radios
throughout the game @gameplay[1:45:30]. The version heard on the radio
features the sound of robotic singing, but Morusque's version contains the
melody and rhythm entirely in the guitar part. This suggests that the piece of
sheet music is actually a cover version, a replaying of an existing piece of
music. The acts of drawing inspiration from both classical music and popular
radio music continues to characterize this diegetic tracks as aspects of a
thriving musical culture, one which draws from the human culture it mirrors,
but is continuing to develop in its own unique manner. 

A unique piece which stand out from Morusque's other performances is 
"Untitled". The soloist describes the piece as containing "exactly 44 notes", 
and proceeds to play exactly 44 notes @gameplay[1:46:32]. The piece has a 
distinct lack of tonal center and rhythm. The wide dispersion of note pitches 
and pace of certain segments communicate Morusque's inhuman playing ability as 
such a piece would require a human player to make extremely fast movements on 
a real guitar. Morusque's lack of surprise at the rule-defying nature of this 
music implies that he interprets it the same a human might interpret, for 
instance, a improvized jazz solo. This follows the same pattern as the radio 
music Van der Cruyssen mentions in his interview with Jeriaska (2023). The 
title "Untitled" also alludes to the manner in which a computer program names 
a file that a human has not yet given a meaningful name. This piece can be 
interpreted as the way in which a crude computer algorithm might react if 
prompted to make "jazz music". Morusque's reaction to the piece, or lack 
thereof, is more telling than the nature of the composition itself. For him, 
this is just another instance of the ingenuity of robot artists and an 
indication of the musicality of the robots' culture.

The fact that an instrumentalist exists at all in the world of _Stray_ is 
narratively significant and has an immersive quality. In the same manner as 
the radio music, the guitar sounds change based on proximity of the player-
character to the guitarist, leading to what Kafili and Brennan describe as 
"spatial awareness" (2024, p. 452). Additionally, the performances are brief 
and personal, so they invite the player to stop and listen, as one would with 
a human street performer, rather than run off with new background music. 
Kafili and Brennan describe this connection as "immersive listening" (p. 455). 
These spatial gameplay elements further enhances the sense of immersion 
created by the other forms of diegetic audio throughout the game. _Stray_ uses 
immersion to amplify the narrative significance of diegetic music. Kafili and 
Brennan continue their exploration of spatial sound design in _Stray_ by 
describing how sound functions and leitmotifs exposit that the game takes 
place in a dystopian and futuristic world (pp. 452-455). In the same way, 
Morusque's performances function as aural exposition about the way in which 
robot culture in the city both imitates the long lost human culture and grows 
as a unique expression of robot existence.
